WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.010
all right, let's get into the polling news.

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:04.002
A lot of people asking me to talk about that,
about

00:00:04.002 --> 00:00:07.840
this and there is a lot of interesting stuff
happening but also a lot of misreporting about

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:08.940
some of what's going on.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:14.579
So there is big news out of the 2020 Democratic
primary, which is that there are now two polls

00:00:14.579 --> 00:00:20.070
which show the Democratic primary to be a
statistical three-way tie between the presumed

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:25.929
front runner at former VP Joe Biden, Vermont
Senator Bernie Sanders and Massachusetts Senator

00:00:25.929 --> 00:00:26.970
Elizabeth Warren.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:32.980
Now the headlines are going crazy with all
sorts of claims that are not backed by the

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:35.210
data and they're ignoring that.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:39.469
There have actually been three recent polls,
one of which paints a very different picture,

00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:40.910
which we shouldn't ignore either.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.600
So let's look at what we have here.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.500
US starting with a new Monmouth poll in the
Monmouth poll.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:53.730
You will see that over the last two months,
Bernie Sanders has jumped from 14 to 20% Elizabeth

00:00:53.730 --> 00:01:04.610
Warren has jumped from 15 to 20% and Joe Biden
has dropped bigly from 32 to 19% this is Biden

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.540
down 13 points, Bernie and Warren gaining.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:14.260
And when you consider the margin of error,
this is statistically at least possibly a

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:17.710
three way tie in the 2028 Democratic primary.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:21.659
Everything else, every other candidate, basically
identical to two months ago.

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:24.759
So this is just one poll and it's a statistical
tie.

00:01:24.759 --> 00:01:30.360
Uh, this is not the only pole, however, that
says that we now have a statistical tie.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:38.619
There is a new economist you gov poll, which
has Biden at 22 Sanders at 19 and Warren at

00:01:38.619 --> 00:01:39.619
17.

00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:43.729
When you account for the margin of error plus
or minus a little more than two and a half

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:50.079
percentage points, you have also potentially
a statistical tie.

00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:54.310
And then, and this is what a lot of people
aren't talking about, the newest poll of all,

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:59.030
uh, is a political poll, which is actually
Biden's still very solidly in the lead.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:04.840
Uh, the new political poll, which is not being
talked about, very says Biden 33 Sanders 20

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:10.260
Warren 15, suggesting that everything is basically
the way it's been a for a month or a little

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:11.260
bit longer.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:18.330
So generally speaking, is the race swinging
more and more away from Joe Biden?

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:19.640
Yes, it is.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:25.830
Is it believable that Joe Biden lost 13 points
over the last two months?

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.680
As one poll says, I don't know.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.460
I, I genuinely don't know.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.590
It seems like a lot.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:37.070
It seems that there's definitely been a shift
away from Biden and as a progressive, I want

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.070
a progressive to win the Democratic nomination.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:44.310
So I'm not saying I'm not glad about it, but
I'm skeptical that Biden really lost 13 points

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:45.780
over the last two months.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:52.130
There are still plenty of polls that have
a Joe Biden ahead, but the big story here,

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:57.150
there's no doubt there is a big story here
in terms of the trend line trend number one,

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.720
it's not going well for Joe Biden.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.530
He was pulling more than 40% when he announced
several months ago.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:11.940
Trend number two, Joe Biden's losses are mostly
helping Bernie and Warren, not, uh, more establishment

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.490
candidates like Kamala Harris and Pete booty
judge.

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:17.390
And I'm going to talk about that in a little
bit.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:22.710
Uh, now at some point this is going to become
a real issue.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:26.090
Are Bernie and Warren splitting the progressive
vote?

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:30.290
I was asked this in an audience question a
month ago, or maybe it was more like six weeks

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:31.290
ago.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:35.560
People had been asking me, shouldn't Bernie
or Warren get out in order to let the other

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.690
progressive consolidate the progressive vote?

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:43.930
Because clearly if Bernie drops, Warren gets
all of his support and if Warren drops, Bernie

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:45.670
gets all of her support.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:47.720
I am not so sure about those numbers.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.450
I was looking yesterday.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:55.880
Yes, if Elizabeth Warren drops out, uh, some
of her support goes to Bernie.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:59.850
If Bernie drops out, some of his support goes
to Warren.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:05.270
Uh, but there are a number of polls which
suggests that the numbers here are a little

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:06.270
bit different.

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:13.330
One survey has Sanders supporters saying their
top second choice is Biden and Warren supporters

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.610
saying their top second choice is Harris.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:22.570
And what this suggests to me is that this
is much more delineated among demographic

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:24.320
lines than ideological.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:30.280
So I don't think we should be too quick to
assume that if Bernie drops his 19% goes to

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:35.500
Warren or if Warren drops her 17% or whatever
goes to Bernie.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.600
Because the polls right now are not suggesting
that that's the case.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.910
But at a certain point there is going to be
a question of will this all work itself out?

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:49.840
It's a good thing to see that the progressive
movement has 35 to 40% support right now just

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:56.280
among Bernie and Warren, but could it at some
point prevent a more progressive candidate

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:57.960
from defeating Joe Biden?

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:02.060
That is a question we're going to be looking
at more this week and into next week.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:07.510
Lastly, there are now 10 people qualified
for the third debate in a couple of weeks.

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:12.720
It's Biden booted, Judge Harris, Sanders,
Warren, booker O'Rourke, clover char, Castro

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.060
and Yang.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:18.000
They've qualified on donor criteria and polling
criteria.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:24.060
We have Tom Stier, Telsey, Gabbert, and Marianne
Williamson qualified on number of donors,

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.230
but not qualified on polling.

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:28.240
They may or may not be in the next debate.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.060
Everybody else will be out.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:33.482
So the number is starting to diminish and
I think that that is actually a good thing.

